    "recommendations": "Reducing the density of shrubs and ground vegetation within the immediate area surrounding the structure could improve defensible space and reduce fuel continuity between the brushland interface and the building. Where vegetation is in direct contact with or growing close to the structure, creating separation through selective thinning may reduce structure exposure and support suppression operations. Managing fuel continuity across the property — particularly between the tree cluster, scattered trees, and ground vegetation — could reduce opportunities for connected fire spread. The long driveway and multiple access routes are notable assets; keeping these routes clear of encroaching vegetation along their margins would support firefighter access and improve evacuation conditions. The water feature present on the property may offer supplemental suppression support and could be identified and made accessible to responding crews. Continued vegetation management that maintains separation between shrub concentrations, trees, and improvements would support long-term wildfire resilience across the property."
